1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,912
Aarav reads the back of
an old phone battery.

2
00:00:03,212 --> 00:00:04,978
Three point seven volts.

3
00:00:04,978 --> 00:00:07,162
Five thousand milliampere hours.

4
00:00:07,462 --> 00:00:11,333
And when he plays a heavy game,
the battery bar drops fast.

5
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:13,860
What do these numbers really mean?

6
00:00:13,860 --> 00:00:14,949
Let's find out.

7
00:00:15,449 --> 00:00:18,412
A cell is like a small
pump for electric charge.

8
00:00:18,762 --> 00:00:21,394
Inside are two metal
plates, called electrodes.

9
00:00:21,744 --> 00:00:25,967
Between them is a chemical paste
or liquid, called the electrolyte.

10
00:00:26,317 --> 00:00:29,688
A chemical reaction pushes electrons
out of the negative terminal.

11
00:00:30,338 --> 00:00:34,468
They travel round the circuit, and
come back in at the positive terminal.

12
00:00:34,818 --> 00:00:38,525
So a cell turns chemical
energy into electrical energy.

13
00:00:39,175 --> 00:00:42,776
Join two or more cells
together, and you get a battery.

14
00:00:43,426 --> 00:00:44,804
Cells come in two families.

15
00:00:45,154 --> 00:00:47,134
Primary cells are used once.

16
00:00:47,134 --> 00:00:49,420
Their reaction cannot be reversed.

17
00:00:49,770 --> 00:00:51,739
A torch dry cell is primary.

18
00:00:51,739 --> 00:00:54,159
It gives about one point five volts.

19
00:00:54,809 --> 00:00:57,943
Secondary cells can be
recharged again and again.

20
00:00:58,293 --> 00:01:00,847
A lead acid cell gives about two volts.

21
00:01:00,847 --> 00:01:03,649
Six of them make a
twelve volt car battery.

22
00:01:03,999 --> 00:01:08,453
A lithium ion cell, like a phone
battery, gives about three point seven

23
00:01:08,453 --> 00:01:09,353
volts.

24
00:01:09,697 --> 00:01:12,862
Now, why does the battery
bar drop during a heavy game?

25
00:01:13,212 --> 00:01:16,492
Every cell has a little
resistance inside it.

26
00:01:16,492 --> 00:01:19,540
We call it internal resistance, small r.

27
00:01:19,890 --> 00:01:22,600
It is like a narrow pipe inside the pump.

28
00:01:22,600 --> 00:01:24,270
Some push is lost there.

29
00:01:24,920 --> 00:01:27,545
Let's test a cell on the breadboard.

30
00:01:27,545 --> 00:01:30,918
EMF one point five volts, r half an ohm.

31
00:01:31,268 --> 00:01:33,505
With no load, no current flows.

32
00:01:33,505 --> 00:01:36,565
The meter reads the full
one point five volts.

33
00:01:36,915 --> 00:01:41,977
This full push, with no current,
is called the EMF, capital E.

34
00:01:42,627 --> 00:01:45,098
Now connect a two point five ohm load.

35
00:01:45,098 --> 00:01:47,080
The current is half an ampere.

36
00:01:47,430 --> 00:01:50,254
The meter drops to one
point two five volts.

37
00:01:50,604 --> 00:01:51,859
A one ohm load.

38
00:01:51,859 --> 00:01:53,722
More current, one ampere.

39
00:01:53,722 --> 00:01:55,859
The voltage falls to one volt.

40
00:01:56,209 --> 00:01:57,426
A half ohm load.

41
00:01:57,426 --> 00:01:59,030
One and a half amperes.

42
00:01:59,030 --> 00:02:01,456
Only zero point seven five volts left.

43
00:02:02,106 --> 00:02:03,163
Watch the scope.

44
00:02:03,163 --> 00:02:05,757
More current, lower
voltage at the terminals.

45
00:02:06,407 --> 00:02:08,433
Let's write the rule step by step.

46
00:02:08,783 --> 00:02:11,891
The EMF drives current
through both resistances.

47
00:02:11,891 --> 00:02:13,752
E equals I times R plus r.

48
00:02:14,452 --> 00:02:17,820
So the current I equals
E divided by R plus r.

49
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:21,146
The voltage at the
terminals is V equals IR.

50
00:02:21,846 --> 00:02:23,190
Put them together.

51
00:02:23,190 --> 00:02:24,867
V equals E minus I r.

52
00:02:25,567 --> 00:02:28,496
I r is the voltage lost inside the cell.

53
00:02:28,496 --> 00:02:32,065
A heavy game draws more
current, so more is lost.

54
00:02:32,715 --> 00:02:34,518
Now the milliampere hours.

55
00:02:34,518 --> 00:02:37,005
This is the capacity of the battery.

56
00:02:37,355 --> 00:02:40,176
It tells how much charge
the battery can deliver.

57
00:02:40,526 --> 00:02:41,426
Current times time.

58
00:02:41,371 --> 00:02:42,682
Milliamperes multiplied by hours.

59
00:02:43,332 --> 00:02:45,977
One milliampere hour is
three point six coulombs.

60
00:02:46,627 --> 00:02:48,327
Cells can also be joined.

61
00:02:48,327 --> 00:02:50,479
In series, the EM Fs add up.

62
00:02:50,829 --> 00:02:53,255
The internal resistances add up too.

63
00:02:53,255 --> 00:02:55,610
So n cells give n E, and n r.

64
00:02:56,260 --> 00:02:59,809
In parallel, identical
cells keep the same EMF.

65
00:03:00,159 --> 00:03:02,825
But the internal
resistance becomes r by n.

66
00:03:02,825 --> 00:03:04,251
And they last longer.

67
00:03:04,901 --> 00:03:06,446
A cell's push is E.

68
00:03:06,446 --> 00:03:08,482
Its inside takes away I r.

69
00:03:09,282 --> 00:03:10,396
Worked example.

70
00:03:10,396 --> 00:03:15,239
A phone battery is three point seven
volts, five thousand milliampere hours.

71
00:03:15,589 --> 00:03:19,296
Five thousand milliampere
hours is five ampere hours.

72
00:03:19,996 --> 00:03:23,316
Charge equals five amperes,
times thirty six hundred seconds.

73
00:03:23,316 --> 00:03:25,255
That is eighteen thousand coulombs.

74
00:03:25,955 --> 00:03:28,380
Energy equals voltage times charge.

75
00:03:28,380 --> 00:03:31,237
Three point seven times eighteen thousand.

76
00:03:31,937 --> 00:03:36,583
That is sixty six thousand six hundred
joules, or eighteen point five watt

77
00:03:36,583 --> 00:03:37,483
hours.

78
00:03:37,895 --> 00:03:42,117
If the phone draws two hundred fifty
milliamperes, it lasts five thousand

79
00:03:42,117 --> 00:03:43,125
by two fifty.

80
00:03:43,125 --> 00:03:44,078
Twenty hours.

81
00:03:44,728 --> 00:03:46,190
Now, the exam trap.

82
00:03:46,540 --> 00:03:50,161
Many students think EMF and
terminal voltage are the same.

83
00:03:50,511 --> 00:03:51,512
They are not.

84
00:03:51,512 --> 00:03:53,051
V equals E minus I r.

85
00:03:53,701 --> 00:03:56,402
They are equal only when no current flows.

86
00:03:57,052 --> 00:03:58,722
Where is this asked?

87
00:03:58,722 --> 00:04:02,354
In PUC, CBSE and ISC board papers.

88
00:04:02,704 --> 00:04:07,273
JEE Main asks it too, with
cells in series and in parallel.

89
00:04:07,623 --> 00:04:11,946
Expect terminal voltage, internal
resistance, and combinations of cells.

90
00:04:12,596 --> 00:04:13,496
Try this.

91
00:04:13,418 --> 00:04:18,155
Four identical cells, each one point
five volts with a quarter ohm inside,

92
00:04:18,155 --> 00:04:20,839
are in series with a five ohm resistor.

93
00:04:20,839 --> 00:04:22,842
What is the terminal voltage?

94
00:04:28,242 --> 00:04:29,142
B.

95
00:04:28,651 --> 00:04:29,569
Five volts.

96
00:04:29,919 --> 00:04:33,668
In series, E is four times
one point five, six volts.

97
00:04:33,668 --> 00:04:35,064
And r is one ohm.

98
00:04:35,414 --> 00:04:38,074
The current is six by five plus one.

99
00:04:38,074 --> 00:04:39,041
One ampere.

100
00:04:39,391 --> 00:04:42,397
So V equals six minus one times one.

101
00:04:42,397 --> 00:04:43,490
Five volts.

102
00:04:44,140 --> 00:04:45,686
Screenshot this card.

103
00:04:48,886 --> 00:04:50,596
Next, soldering and desoldering.

104
00:04:50,596 --> 00:04:53,096
How do you join parts so
they never come loose?
